# Every declared site under `api/src` that carries a natural person's email address as a parameter or a
# promoted constructor property, OUTSIDE the fixed set `SensitiveRecipientAddressGateTest` already pins by the
# parameter name `recipientEmail`. This registry exists because that name-keyed gate cannot generalise (#769):
# the same value crosses this codebase under `$email`, `$to`, `$identifier`, `$emailIdentifier`, `$raw` and
# `$value`, and a rule keyed on any single name undercounts by construction — measured at 24 sites reachable by
# name against >=31 reachable by reading what a method actually does with its argument.
#
# The rule this file exists to enforce is the same one `SensitiveRecipientAddressGateTest` already enforces for
# `recipientEmail`: a person's address that can reach a stack-trace frame must be declared
# `#[SensitiveParameter]`, so it renders as `Object(SensitiveParameterValue)` rather than in clear the day
# `zend.exception_ignore_args` (the control that holds the line TODAY, unconditionally, for every frame
# including vendor ones — see that gate) is ever weakened. Marking the parameter costs nothing and does not
# depend on the parameter's declared TYPE or on whether it satisfies a third-party interface: PHP's argument
# redaction operates on the concrete frame actually on the stack, not on an interface it happens to implement,
# so `UserProvider::loadUserByIdentifier(string $identifier)` can carry the attribute directly even though it
# cannot be RETYPED away from `string` without breaking Symfony's `UserProviderInterface` contract.
#
# Format, one per line:
#   <Fqcn>::<method>($<param>)  => sensitive
#   <Fqcn>::<method>($<param>)  => excluded :: <reason>
#
# `sensitive` requires `#[SensitiveParameter]` at that declaration. `excluded` is a conscious, reviewed
# statement that this particular value is not a person's address DESPITE its shape — an env-derived
# operational mailbox address is the only kind used here — and it is deliberately not a silent omission: a
# site with no line at all is what `PersonAddressParameterGateTest::theRegistryClassifiesOnlyRecognisedVerdicts`
# and its completeness check both refuse.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What this registry's COMPLETENESS actually rests on, read as the specification of the gate's blind spots —
# a green build proves what is below the line, and nothing above it.
#
#   - THE MECHANICALLY-DISCOVERED HALF IS NARROW BY DESIGN. The gate automatically requires a line for every
#     parameter or promoted property in `api/src` typed exactly `Email` (or `?Email`,
#     `Erpify\Iam\Identity\Domain\Email`) — that direction cannot go stale in the completeness sense, because
#     reflection finds it whether or not a human remembered to add a line. Today that population may well be
#     EMPTY: every site below was found holding the value as a bare `string`, which is exactly the shape the
#     name-keyed gate and a type-keyed rule (`Email`-only) both fail to reach — see #769's second comment,
#     which rejected a pure type-based rule for precisely this reason. The automatic half exists so that the
#     day a caller DOES start typing a parameter `Email`, marking it is enforced rather than optional; it is
#     not, today, doing most of the file's actual work.
#   - EVERY STRING-TYPED SITE BELOW WAS FOUND BY ADVERSARIAL REVIEW, NOT BY THIS GATE, AND THE NEXT ONE WILL BE
#     TOO. There is no mechanical predicate in this repository's toolchain (no dataflow/taint engine — see
#     `api/CLAUDE.md`'s note on Psalm's removal and Semgrep's narrow, three-flow ruleset) that can soundly
#     trace an arbitrary `string $foo` to "this becomes a person's address three calls later" without either
#     false-negating real sites or false-positiving every other `string` parameter in the tree. The two
#     comments on #769 that produced most of this file's population were found by a human reading call graphs,
#     which is the same control `../CLAUDE.md`'s "No security, GDPR, or audit-surface work opens a PR without a
#     recorded adversarial pass" already mandates for exactly this class of change. This file is where that
#     review's findings get a permanent, reviewable, gated home instead of living only in an issue thread — it
#     is not a replacement for the review that finds them.
#   - IT CANNOT JUDGE THE CLASSIFICATION, and the two directions of error are not symmetric here either.
#     Writing `excluded` over a site that does carry a real person's address is not self-refuting the way it is
#     in `.person-reference-policy` (there, a wrong `person` line needs an erasure file that doesn't exist and
#     the build stops) — there is no analogous forcing function here, so `excluded` passes on the classifier's
#     word alone. `NotificationMailer::send($to)` and `PlainTextNotificationMailer::send($to)` are deliberately
#     classified `sensitive` rather than `excluded` for this exact reason: today's only caller passes an
#     env-derived operations mailbox, but the port is published, and the issue that found this crossing (#769,
#     second comment) is explicit that the safety is "a property of the current call graph, not of the
#     parameter" — an exclusion here would silently stop being true the day a second caller passes a real
#     person's address, and nothing would notice.
#   - ANONYMOUS CLASSES, CLOSURES AND PLAIN FUNCTIONS are unreachable by the same limitation
#     `SensitiveRecipientAddressGateTest` already documents: the walk derives a type from a file's PSR-4 path.
#   - IT SAYS NOTHING ABOUT `getMessage()`, which is where an address actually reaches a Monolog sink today —
#     that axis is the mail boundary (`RedactingMailer`/`RedactingTransport`), not this one. This registry is
#     purely the frame-ARGUMENT axis: defence in depth for the day `zend.exception_ignore_args` is off, or for
#     a sink (unlike Monolog's `NormalizerFormatter`) that does read frame arguments.
#   - IT DOES NOT COVER VENDOR FRAMES. `SmtpTransport::doRcptToCommand(string $address)` and similar carry the
#     same string and cannot be marked at all — only `zend.exception_ignore_args` reaches those.
#
# The gate (PersonAddressParameterGateTest) checks four mechanical things: every line still names a real,
# currently-declared site (staleness); every `sensitive` line's site carries `#[SensitiveParameter]`
# (enforcement); every `Email`-typed parameter or promoted property in `api/src` has a line, of either verdict
# (completeness of the narrow automatic half); and every line's verdict is exactly `sensitive` or
# `excluded :: <non-empty reason>` (no silent typo reading as neither). It runs via the unfiltered
# `make php.unit` / `make php.unit.coverage` (CI gates on the latter) — like its sibling
# `SensitiveRecipientAddressGateTest`, it has no dedicated `php.lint.*` target, so `make php.quality` does NOT
# exercise it; run `make php.unit` locally, or filter with `--filter=PersonAddressParameterGateTest`.

